A project keeps your manuscript and notes together. Here is how to start writing.

## Create your first project

1. In the opening window (the *Launcher*), click *New Work*.
2. Under *Details*, enter a name and choose where to save it. Choose *Single file* to keep
   the project in one `.skrib` file. The author name is optional.
3. Under *Language & structure*, choose the language you write in. You can leave the other
   settings as they are for now.
4. Under *Template*, choose *Empty Novel* for a novel you want to build chapter by chapter.
   Other templates give you more chapters to start with; *Notebook* is for notes.
5. Click *Create Work*.

## Write your first scene

The *Binder* panel on the left lists your chapters, scenes and notes. Click a scene to open
it and start writing. To add one, select a chapter and use the create button at the top of
*Binder*. Its menu lets you choose *Scene* or another kind of item.

Press `Enter` to start a new paragraph. To start a new line in the same paragraph, for the
lines of a poem, a song or a letter, press `Shift+Enter`, or choose *Format ▸ Insert line
break*.

Beside the main text is the *Synopsis*: a short summary you can use to plan the scene.
You can hide it in *Settings ▸ Editor ▸ Editor Behavior*.

To rename a scene or chapter, right-click it and choose *Rename*.

## Save and reopen your work

Press `Ctrl+S` to save your changes. If you have enabled autosave in Settings, Skribisto
saves as you write and hides the manual Save command. The save indicator in the status bar
shows whether your changes have been saved.

Next time you open Skribisto, choose your project from *Recent Works*. Use *Open* to find
an existing `.skrib` file instead.

Skribisto also makes [backups](:help-backups-and-versions), which let you recover earlier work.

## Bring in existing writing

In the Launcher, *Create from…* starts a project from
[documents](:help-import-documents), a [Scrivener project](:help-import-scrivener), or a
[Manuskript or Plume Creator project](:help-import-projects).
To explore a sample project, open *Examples* in the sidebar.

## Adjust your workspace

- Press `F9`, or choose *View ▸ Outline*, to show or hide the Binder.
- Press `Ctrl+Enter` on a selected item to open it in the other editor pane.
- When creating a project, *Flat chapters* lets you write directly in each chapter instead
  of dividing it into scenes. *Bundle* saves the project as a folder instead of a single file.

See [books, chapters and scenes](:help-writing-model) for more about organizing your manuscript.
